  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "No quota API — so allowances are derived",
      "items": [
        "Probed: no plan upstream returns rate-limit headers or a balance endpoint, so the kit derives allowances from what it controls — measured weighted spend plus your console readings.",
        "Every upstream call is metered at one chokepoint: tokens land in hourly buckets per provider plus a cumulative weighted counter; costUsd comes only from declared prices, and trigger records who spent (operator or app:<name>).",
        "offpeak { from, to, weight, tz }: off-peak hours count at the declared fraction, so the conversion stays stable as your model mix shifts. A recent ring (~100 requests) keeps latency and error rate per model."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Declared + calibrated — estimates with a known direction of error",
      "items": [
        "A console read (date, % used) is stamped with cumulative weighted spend at that instant; allowance ≈ Δspend ÷ Δpct, headroom = 1 − spend ÷ allowance.",
        "Spend is a windowed weighted sum: pool (declared start) · calendar (billing day anchor) · rolling (trailing windowHours).",
        "A % that drops between reads is a reset — never calibrated across it. The latest pair wins; earlier pairs only report a stability spread.",
        "These are estimates, reconciled at every new console read (ledger-implied % vs the next read). Steering only reorders candidates the tier already trusts — a bad estimate costs a suboptimal pick, never quality outside the tier."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Neutral by default",
      "items": [
        "Providers without a quota declaration pass untouched: balancing never diverts away from the unknown, only toward what is known to have room.",
        "The dashboard lists every routed but undeclared provider as neutral, and the quota tab stays hidden (SHOW_QUOTA_TAB) until real console readings exist — steering works as soon as the roster declares quota."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Fail open, everywhere",
      "items": [
        "No readings → steering still works; exhausted-anyway candidates fall to the runtime walk, where quota vocabulary is matched before the 429 pattern so an exhausted window benches the provider — never the key.",
        "No spend table, a budget-gate refusal, or an empty answer ends the forecast loop with the refusal verbatim: routing unchanged, nothing retried.",
        "The loop only reads. An owner answer is what re-routes tiers — before anything dies mid-task."
      ]
    }
  ]
